From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 08:14:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] defer automatic turns behind proven updating marker
 owners

Five whys:
1. An automatic turn can display an ownership card while a core update is
   still holding the paused turn's live writer.
2. Admission returns SESSION_NOT_OWNED for this temporary custody too.
3. The automatic-turn publication gate previously distinguished the gateway
   ledger, but not positively proven updating marker owners.
4. Registry refusals discarded the holder's recorded process incarnation,
   leaving consumers unable to distinguish that case from a foreign writer.
5. Coverage asserted exclusion and card throttling, not update-bound silent
   deferral with install, incarnation and kernel-custody controls.

Carry registry PID/birth without changing lease acquisition or release.
Classify a labelled copy only when the recorded holder matches a paused
marker, the verified same-install spawn ledger, a live updater incarnation,
and real POSIX checkout kernel-lock contention. Automatic submissions keep
existing retry/backoff but do not emit a takeover card for that case.
Typed, foreign, unknown, stale and non-updating cases retain the card.

Historical limit: earlier logs identify marker writer PIDs, not their recorded
birth/install provenance. They cannot be retroactively certified. Durable
PID-only lease holders and unprovable/Windows lock observations retain the
existing fail-closed behavior; this does not reclaim or release any lease.

Proof before commit:
- Vertical canonical RED/GREEN slices for classifier, registry PID/birth,
  automatic card suppression, producer wiring and foreign warning cause.
- Real separate-process writer + marker + registry + spawn ledger + update
  lock integration, retry/recovery, and fail-closed matrix pass canonically.
- scripts/check --base origin/main: all 21 checks pass.
- Wider consumers and mutation verification follow on this frozen revision;
  independent exact-head review and required CI remain landing gates.
